% Solutions to the Chapter 2 exercises.
accumulate pond.

pred above o:critter, o:critter.
above X Y :- eats X Y.
above X Z :- eats X Y, above Y Z.

% ANCHOR: ex1
% Exercise 2.1: a swimmer that eats a swimmer.
% Only the otter: it swims, and it eats the fish, which swims.
pred swimming_hunter o:critter.
swimming_hunter X :- swims X, eats X Y, swims Y.
pred ex1.
ex1 :- std.findall (swimming_hunter X) A, print A.
% ANCHOR_END: ex1

% ANCHOR: ex2
% Exercise 2.2: below is above with the places swapped.
% Fourteen answers. A critter appears once for every path
% down to the algae: the heron has four paths, and the frog,
% the fish, the duck and the otter have two each.
pred below o:critter, o:critter.
below X Y :- above Y X.
pred ex2.
ex2 :- std.findall (below algae W) A, print A.
% ANCHOR_END: ex2

% ANCHOR: ex3
% Exercise 2.3: two critters that eat the same food.
% The frog is its own rival: nothing in the rule says X and Y
% must differ, and the frog certainly eats what the frog eats.
pred rivals o:critter, o:critter.
rivals X Y :- eats X F, eats Y F.
pred ex3.
ex3 :- std.findall (rivals frog W) A, print A.
% ANCHOR_END: ex3

% ANCHOR: ex4
% Exercise 2.4: linked follows eats in both directions.
% `linked frog algae` succeeds: frog to fly to algae.
% `linked frog otter` is true in the picture (frog, heron, fish,
% otter), but the engine never says so. It walks frog, fly,
% algae, snail, algae, snail, ... forever: a step from algae to
% snail and back does not move toward a way out. With --fuel it
% stops with "resource error: out of fuel". Chapter 4's lists
% give a fix: remember where you have been.
pred connected o:critter, o:critter.
connected X Y :- eats X Y.
connected X Y :- eats Y X.
pred linked o:critter, o:critter.
linked X Y :- connected X Y.
linked X Z :- connected X Y, linked Y Z.
pred ex4.
ex4 :- linked frog algae, print "linked frog algae".
% ANCHOR_END: ex4

main :- ex1, ex2, ex3, ex4.
